Add async version of wrap_tool_call for middleware with checks for passed async function and `await handler`. Tested and verified with my own agent through the following snippet:
```
@awrap_tool_call
async def handle_tool_errors(request, handler):
    try:
        return await handler(request)
    except Exception as e:
        return ToolMessage(
            content=f"Tool error: Please check your input and try again. ({str(e)})",
            tool_call_id=request.tool_call["id"],
            is_error=True
        )
```
LinkedIn: https://linkedin.com/in/astroa7m

We previously had a hack where we'd take the first text block.

This isn't suited to model calls nowadays, where we generally expect multiple text blocks in a single invocation. For example when using Opus 4.6 with adaptive thinking, we can get output of the form:
```python
[
  {
    "text": "\n\n",
    "type": "text"
  },
  {
    "signature": "...",
    "thinking": 'The user said "Hello"...',
    "type": "thinking"
  },
  {
    "text": '{"response":"Hello! How can I help you today?"}',
    "type": "text"
  }
]
```
(Note: I verified that the raw response from Anthropic in the above case genuinely contains `TextBlock(citations=None, text='\n\n', type='text')` as the first content element.)

This was mentioned in a comment [here](https://github.com/langchain-ai/langchain/issues/32120#issuecomment-3892808734).

The impact here is that output parsers do not access the full text content, only the first block. So structured output can fail.

Add dynamic `jump_to` support for tools in the agent loop, allowing tools to control agent flow by returning `Command(update={"jump_to": "end"})` to exit the loop immediately without calling the model again.

This enables tools to dynamically decide whether to continue the agent loop or terminate based on runtime conditions (e.g., validation results), providing more flexibility than the static `return_direct=True` option.

Code example
```
@tool
def validate_and_finish(data) -> Command[dict[str, Any]]:
    """Validate data and finish if valid."""
    if verify(data):
        # Stop the agent loop - don't call model again
        return Command(
            update={
                "messages": [ToolMessage(content="verification success")],
                "jump_to": "end",
            }
        )
    else:
        return "Failed to verify ..."
```
